---
// F5 and F7, the mission of Ch. 6: from a random start, after a while every node the same colour. F5: a funnel of
// numbers, each with a picture beside it: all 262 144 rules (two rings), the 512 that are their own cousin (symmetry: either
// colour may win) and the 27 whose average next density moves away from half and half (each rule's two curves, in the
// place where it will run), found without a simulation; then all 27 race on one rewired grid from one start (the network
// and start of F7), within the thesis's 100 rounds: a few agree, the simple threshold rule is stuck, and no other rule
// that is its own cousin agrees there: a strong filter, the conclusion under the funnel once the race is over. F7:
// the winner at work on that grid, the threshold rule on the same start, a slider that rewires the grid for the reader
// (it fails on the grid itself and on a random network), our result, and the question the next chapter answers. Data:
// src/data/defence/consensus.json (scripts/defence/precompute.ts); the run and its verdict: src/scripts/defence/agree.ts.
import Slide from './Slide.astro';
import SimButton from './SimButton.astro';
import { texify, phi } from './tex';
// F5: the two rings, in design pixels of their canvas (src/scripts/defence/donut.ts draws them)
const f5rings = [{ x: 255, y: 235, R: 195, w: 80, on: false }, { x: 755, y: 235, R: 195, w: 80, on: true }];
---
<Slide id="ch-synchronisation" label="The mission: looking for consensus" builds={5} class="f5" ch={6}>
  <h2>The mission: looking for consensus</h2>
  <div class="row grow f5-row">
    <div class="funnel">
      <div class="tier" data-b="1"><b>262&#8239;144</b></div>
      <div class="tier" data-b="2"><b>512</b></div>
      <div class="tier last" data-b="3"><b>27</b></div>
      <p class="concl">Strong filter:<br />metrics inform behaviour</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pane">
      <div class="f5-rings" data-b-only="1 2">
        <canvas class="rings" data-rings={JSON.stringify(f5rings)} aria-label="A rule as two rings: where a node that is off is born, and where a node that is on survives"></canvas>
        <p class="sym" data-b="2"><b>symmetry</b><button type="button" class="ctl f5-cousin" data-cousin>⇄ its cousin</button></p>
      </div>
      <div class="race" data-b="3">
        <div class="race-box">
          <canvas class="tiles" data-b="4" aria-label="The 27 rules, each on the same rewired grid of 900 nodes from the same start"></canvas>
          <canvas class="mf" data-b-only="3" aria-label="The 27 rules, each in its place with its two curves: the average next density in blue and the average next defect in red, each with its tangent"></canvas>
          <div data-b="4"><SimButton class="f5-btn" /></div>
        </div>
        <p class="race-key" data-b="4"><span class="key thr">simple threshold</span><span class="key win">the winner</span><b data-count></b></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">Can we really use what a rule looks like to predict what it will do? A test, in the form of a mission: looking for consensus. From a random start, after a while every node should have the same colour, all on or all off. The obvious candidates are threshold rules: do what most of your neighbours do. On a network they get stuck, in patches that each agree with themselves but never with each other.</p>
  <p slot="say" set:html={texify('There are 262 144 rules at resolution 9: every way of colouring the two rings, here the Game of Life. First filter: we do not care which colour wins, only that both are equally likely, so the rule must treat on and off alike: symmetry, it must be its own cousin, like the simple threshold rule shown here (the button says so). That leaves 512. Second filter, on the average next density: from half on and half off, the network must move away, towards all on or all off. That leaves 27, about one rule in ten thousand, found in a fraction of a second without a single simulation. Here each of them stands where it will run, with the two curves behind the numbers of before, just for a glance (the thesis has the details): in blue the average next density from any start, in red the average next defect, each with its tangent. Every blue curve leaves one half more steeply than the diagonal, so from half and half the network is pushed away; the red tangent is the Boolean sensitivity.')} />
  <p slot="say" set:html={texify('Now we can afford to run every one of them. One click puts them in place, the next runs them: all 27 on the same grid of 900 nodes, a fifth of its links rewired, from the same random start, for at most 100 timesteps, as in the thesis. Most of them freeze in patches within a few rounds, or keep churning, and fade; the simple threshold rule is stuck from timestep 10. Three reach agreement, and they are the only ones: of all 512 rules that treat on and off alike, no other reaches a lasting agreement in this race. A strong filter: the metrics inform the behaviour, and a run decides which rule is best. Run over hundreds of networks, one of them clearly is: $\phi^9_{488,464}$, which brought more than 96% of the rewired grids of the thesis to agreement. It is among the most sensitive of the 27, but sensitivity alone is not enough (Chapter 6, Physica D 2025).')} />
</Slide>

<Slide label="Watch it work" builds={2} class="f7" ch={6}>
  <div class="row grow f7-row">
    <div class="f7-left">
      <div class="f7-box">
        <canvas class="grid" aria-label="The chosen rule on a rewired 30 by 30 grid"></canvas>
        <SimButton class="f7-btn" />
      </div>
      <label class="f7-p">rewired<input type="range" class="ctl" min="-3.3" max="0" step="0.01" value="-0.7" data-p aria-label="share of links rewired: none at the left end, then on a logarithmic scale" /><output data-pv>20%</output></label>
    </div>
    <div class="col f7-side">
      <h2>Watch it work</h2>
      <div class="f7-rules" role="group" aria-label="Rule">
        <button type="button" class="ctl" data-rule="winner" aria-pressed="true">The winner <span set:html={phi(9, 488, 464)} /></button>
        <button type="button" class="ctl" data-rule="threshold" aria-pressed="false">Simple threshold <span set:html={phi(9, 480, 496)} /></button>
      </div>
      <canvas class="trace" aria-label="Share of nodes on, timestep by timestep"></canvas>
      <p class="result" data-b="2"><b>Our result</b> Filter for rules that do what we want</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p slot="say">The winner at work, on the grid of the race (900 nodes, eight neighbours each, a fifth of the links rewired), from the same random start with half of the nodes on. Patches form and fight, and from timestep 31 every node is on. The curve on the right is the share of nodes that are on, timestep by timestep. The buttons switch to the simple threshold rule, on the same network from the same start: within a dozen timesteps it freezes into patches, each content with its own majority, a few nodes blinking at the borders.</p>
  <p slot="say">The slider under the grid rewires it: none at the left end, then on a logarithmic scale from one link in a thousand to all of them; back at 20% it is the race’s grid again. Moved while the rule runs, it only swaps the links: every node keeps its colour and the run goes on (one that was stuck may come loose). The talk stays there: on rewired grids like this one, the winner brought more than 96% of the networks of the thesis to agreement at a fifth rewired, but only 21% of the grids without rewiring and 37% of the fully rewired ones. On this grid and from this start it agrees within the thesis’s 100 timesteps from about 2% to 50% rewired, and not below or above. The thesis ran 900 samples for every setting, on rewired rings, rewired grids and scale-free networks; these grids are a small illustration.</p>
  <p slot="say">Our result: with the metrics as a filter, we find rules that do what we want before running a single one. (To say out loud, not on the slide: which raises the question the other way round. Can we start from rules that already do something we want, such as telling networks apart, and find out what makes them work?)</p>
</Slide>

<style>
  .f5-row { gap: calc(64 * var(--u)); align-items: flex-start; margin-top: calc(100 * var(--u)); }
  .funnel { width: calc(560 * var(--u)); flex: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; }
  .tier { display: flex; justify-content: center; padding: calc(16 * var(--u)) calc(20 * var(--u)); border: calc(3 * var(--u)) solid var(--line); background: var(--panel); position: relative; }
  .tier:nth-child(1) { width: 100%; }
  .tier:nth-child(2) { width: 80%; }
  .tier:nth-child(3) { width: 60%; }
  .tier b { font-size: calc(80 * var(--u)); line-height: 1.1; }
  .tier + .tier { margin-top: calc(48 * var(--u)); }
  /* the funnel narrows: an arrow down into each tier */
  .tier + .tier::before { content: ''; position: absolute; top: calc(-42 * var(--u)); left: 50%; transform: translateX(-50%); border: calc(16 * var(--u)) solid transparent; border-top-color: var(--accent); }
  .tier.last { background: var(--ink); border-color: var(--ink); color: var(--sun); }
  /* the conclusion, once the race is over */
  .concl { font-size: calc(38 * var(--u)); font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.3; text-align: center; margin-top: calc(36 * var(--u)); transition: opacity 0.4s ease; }
  :global(.f5:not(.raced)) .concl { opacity: 0; }
  /* one picture at a time, all in the place of the race */
  .pane { flex: 1; display: grid; }
  .pane > * { grid-area: 1 / 1; }
  .f5-rings { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: calc(16 * var(--u)); }
  .rings { width: calc(1010 * var(--u)); height: calc(470 * var(--u)); }
  .sym { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: calc(36 * var(--u)); }
  /* the word's middle (its x-height, not its line box) on the middle of the button beside it */
  .sym b { font-size: calc(56 * var(--u)); color: var(--accent); transform: translateY(-0.15em); }
  .f5-cousin { font-size: calc(34 * var(--u)); min-width: calc(330 * var(--u)); }
  .race { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: calc(12 * var(--u)); }
  .race-box { position: relative; }
  .tiles { width: calc(1010 * var(--u)); height: calc(584 * var(--u)); display: block; }
  /* the curves in the tiles' place, over them */
  .mf { position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; width: calc(1010 * var(--u)); height: calc(584 * var(--u)); }
  .race-box :global(.f5-btn) { right: calc(-6 * var(--u)); bottom: calc(10 * var(--u)); }
  /* a fixed height, so nothing moves when the winner and the count come in */
  .race-key { font-size: calc(32 * var(--u)); display: flex; align-items: center; gap: calc(36 * var(--u)); height: calc(56 * var(--u)); }
  .key { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: calc(12 * var(--u)); }
  .key::before { content: ''; width: calc(26 * var(--u)); height: calc(26 * var(--u)); border: calc(4 * var(--u)) solid var(--ink); }
  .key.win::before { border-color: var(--danger); border-width: calc(5 * var(--u)); }
  .key.win { color: var(--danger); font-weight: 700; }
  [data-count] { margin-left: auto; font-size: calc(40 * var(--u)); line-height: 1; }
  .key.win, [data-count] { transition: opacity 0.4s ease; }
  :global(.f5:not(.raced)) .key.win, :global(.f5:not(.raced)) [data-count] { opacity: 0; }

  .f7-row { gap: calc(60 * var(--u)); align-items: center; }
  .f7-left { flex: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: calc(40 * var(--u)); }
  .f7-box { position: relative; }
  :global(.f7) .grid { width: calc(740 * var(--u)); height: calc(740 * var(--u)); display: block; }
  /* the slider stops short of the grid's ▶ */
  .f7-p { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: calc(20 * var(--u)); width: calc(660 * var(--u)); font-size: calc(36 * var(--u)); color: var(--muted); }
  .f7-p input { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }
  .f7-p output { min-width: 3.6em; }
  .f7-box :global(.f7-btn) { right: calc(-32 * var(--u)); bottom: calc(-24 * var(--u)); }
  .f7-side { gap: calc(24 * var(--u)); flex: 1; }
  .f7-rules { display: flex; gap: calc(14 * var(--u)); flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .f7-rules .ctl { font-size: calc(30 * var(--u)); padding: calc(8 * var(--u)) calc(20 * var(--u)); }
  .f7-rules .ctl[aria-pressed="true"] { background: var(--ink); border-color: var(--ink); color: var(--sun); }
  .trace { width: 100%; height: calc(260 * var(--u)); }
  /* room to breathe: the result alone under the trace */
  :global(.f7) .result { font-size: calc(48 * var(--u)); padding: calc(30 * var(--u)) calc(36 * var(--u)); margin-top: calc(30 * var(--u)); }
</style>

<script>
  import { slide, sim } from '../../scripts/defence/deck';
  import { stage, onSize, ticker } from '../../scripts/defence/stage';
  import { axes, curve, label, px, py, type Plot } from '../../scripts/defence/plot';
  import { picker, type Ring } from '../../scripts/defence/donut';
  import { cousin } from '../../scripts/defence/rings';
  import { curveOf } from '../../scripts/defence/curves';
  import { hue, tangent } from '../../scripts/defence/cobweb';
  import { makeRng, lattice } from '../../scripts/net';
  import { type Rule, randomState, density } from '../../scripts/llna';
  import { agreement, WINNER, THRESHOLD, type Agreement, type Outcome } from '../../scripts/defence/agree';
  import { parse } from '../../scripts/palette';
  import data from '../../data/defence/consensus.json';

  const cands = data.candidates as { B: number; S: number; bs8: number; slope: number }[];
  const is = (c: { B: number; S: number }, r: Rule) => c.B === r.B && c.S === r.S;
  // the rewired grid of the mission (eight neighbours each, a fifth of the links rewired) and the start, for F5 and F7
  const SIDE = 30, P_REWIRE = 0.2, demo = data.demo;
  const demoNet = () => lattice(SIDE, 8, P_REWIRE, makeRng(demo.netSeed)), demoStart = () => randomState(SIDE * SIDE, 0.5, makeRng(demo.startSeed));

  // ── F5: a funnel of numbers, a picture beside each in the place of the race: the rings of the Game of Life (every rule
  // is a way of colouring them; a click on an interval changes it), then the simple threshold rule, which is its own cousin
  // (symmetry), then each of the 27 with its two curves in its tile; then the 27 race on the same grid from the same start,
  // five rounds a second, for at most the thesis's 100 rounds. A tile that reaches agreement is framed; one that freezes or is still churning at the end
  // fades. The simple threshold rule is ringed from the start, the winner once the race is over. The start is fixed:
  // the speaker text quotes the outcome, and F7 replays two of the tiles. ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.f5')) {
    const c = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.tiles')!, count = s.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-count]')!;
    const COLS = 7, GAP = 22, PAD = 12, LIMIT = 100;
    const net = demoNet(), first = demoStart();
    let runs: Agreement[] = [], verdict: (Outcome | null)[] = [];
    // one 30 × 30 bitmap per tile, scaled up without smoothing: square cells
    const bits = cands.map(() => { const o = document.createElement('canvas'); o.width = o.height = SIDE; return o; });
    const over = () => {
      const done = verdict.every(Boolean);
      s.classList.toggle('raced', done);
      count.textContent = done ? `${verdict.filter((v) => v!.ok).length} of 27 agree` : '';
    };
    const reset = () => { runs = cands.map((r) => agreement(net, { r: 9, B: r.B, S: r.S }, first, LIMIT)); verdict = runs.map(() => null); over(); };
    function paint() {
      const st = stage(c); if (!st) return;
      const { ctx, t } = st, T = (st.w - 2 * PAD - (COLS - 1) * GAP) / COLS;
      const on = parse(t.ink), off = parse(t.panel), done = verdict.every(Boolean);
      ctx.imageSmoothingEnabled = false;
      cands.forEach((r, i) => {
        const x = PAD + (i % COLS) * (T + GAP), y = PAD + Math.floor(i / COLS) * (T + GAP), v = verdict[i], a = runs[i].state;
        const o = bits[i], octx = o.getContext('2d')!, img = octx.createImageData(SIDE, SIDE);
        for (let k = 0; k < a.length; k++) { const col = a[k] ? on : off; img.data[4 * k] = col[0]; img.data[4 * k + 1] = col[1]; img.data[4 * k + 2] = col[2]; img.data[4 * k + 3] = 255; }
        octx.putImageData(img, 0, 0);
        ctx.globalAlpha = v && !v.ok ? 0.22 : 1;
        ctx.drawImage(o, x, y, T, T);
        ctx.strokeStyle = t.line; ctx.lineWidth = 1.5; ctx.strokeRect(x, y, T, T);
        ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
        if (v?.ok) { ctx.strokeStyle = t.accent; ctx.lineWidth = 6; ctx.strokeRect(x - 3, y - 3, T + 6, T + 6); }
        if (is(r, THRESHOLD)) { ctx.strokeStyle = t.ink; ctx.lineWidth = 4; ctx.strokeRect(x - 8, y - 8, T + 16, T + 16); }
        if (is(r, WINNER) && done) { ctx.strokeStyle = t.danger; ctx.lineWidth = 5; ctx.strokeRect(x - 8, y - 8, T + 16, T + 16); }
      });
    }
    const race = ticker(s, 5, () => {
      runs.forEach((r, i) => { if (!verdict[i]) verdict[i] = r.next(); });
      const done = verdict.every(Boolean);
      if (done) over();
      return done;
    }, paint);
    sim(s.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]')!, 5, { reset() { race.play(false); reset(); paint(); }, play() { race.play(true); } });

    // the rings: the Game of Life up to the first filter, the simple threshold rule from it; the button names the cousin
    const rc = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.rings')!, swap = s.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-cousin]')!;
    const LIFE: Rule = { r: 9, B: 8, S: 12 };
    const rules = picker(rc, JSON.parse(rc.dataset.rings!) as [Ring, Ring], LIFE, (x) => {
      const twin = cousin(x), own = twin.B === x.B && twin.S === x.S;
      swap.toggleAttribute('data-own', own); swap.textContent = own ? 'its own cousin' : '⇄ its cousin';
    });
    swap.addEventListener('click', () => rules.cousin());

    // each rule's two curves in its tile (eight neighbours each), as on the metrics slides but without words: the average
    // next density in blue, with its tangent at ½ (how unstable), and the average next defect in red, with its tangent at
    // the origin (how sensitive); a frame, no diagonal; the simple threshold rule ringed, as in the race
    const mf = s.querySelector<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas.mf')!;
    const pairs = cands.map((r) => ({ d: curveOf({ r: 9, B: r.B, S: r.S }, 'density'), e: curveOf({ r: 9, B: r.B, S: r.S }, 'defect'), r }));
    const paintCurves = () => {
      const st = stage(mf); if (!st) return;
      const { ctx, t } = st, T = (st.w - 2 * PAD - (COLS - 1) * GAP) / COLS, IN = 10, DASH = [7, 6];
      pairs.forEach(({ d, e, r }, i) => {
        const x = PAD + (i % COLS) * (T + GAP), y = PAD + Math.floor(i / COLS) * (T + GAP);
        ctx.fillStyle = t.panel; ctx.fillRect(x, y, T, T);
        ctx.strokeStyle = t.line; ctx.lineWidth = 1.5; ctx.strokeRect(x, y, T, T);
        const q: Plot = { x: x + IN, y: y + IN, w: T - 2 * IN, h: T - 2 * IN, x0: 0, x1: 1, y0: 0, y1: 1 };
        tangent(st, q, { x: 0.5, y: 0.5, slope: r.slope }, hue(t, 'density'), 2.5, DASH);
        tangent(st, q, { x: 0, y: 0, slope: r.bs8 }, hue(t, 'defect'), 2.5, DASH);
        curve(st, q, d, hue(t, 'density'), 4);
        curve(st, q, e, hue(t, 'defect'), 4);
        if (is(r, THRESHOLD)) { ctx.strokeStyle = t.ink; ctx.lineWidth = 4; ctx.strokeRect(x - 8, y - 8, T + 16, T + 16); }
      });
    };

    slide(s, {
      set(b: number) { rules.reset(b >= 2 ? THRESHOLD : LIFE); },
      leave() { race.play(false); },
    });
    reset();
    onSize(c, paint); onSize(mf, paintCurves);
  }

  // ── F7: the winner (or the threshold rule) on the race's grid. The slider under it rewires the grid: none at its left
  // end, then on a logarithmic scale from one link in a thousand to all of them, always with the race's seed, so 20% is
  // the race's grid again; the slide opens there. A rewiring while the rule runs keeps every node's state: only the
  // links change, and a run that was stuck goes on. ──
  for (const s of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.slide.f7')) {
    const [grid, trace] = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLCanvasElement>('canvas'));
    const side = SIDE, N = side * side, first = demoStart();
    const slider = s.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-p]')!, pv = s.querySelector<HTMLOutputElement>('[data-pv]')!;
    let p = P_REWIRE, net = demoNet();
    /** The share the slider points at, to two significant digits (so 20% is exactly the race's fifth), 0 below a
     * thousandth; true if it changed. */
    const rewire = () => {
      const v = +slider.value, q = v < -3.15 ? 0 : Number((10 ** Math.max(-3, v)).toPrecision(2));
      pv.textContent = `${+(q * 100).toPrecision(2)}%`;
      if (q === p) return false;
      p = q; net = lattice(side, 8, p, makeRng(demo.netSeed));
      return true;
    };
    const RULES: Record<string, Rule> = { winner: WINNER, threshold: THRESHOLD };
    const buttons = Array.from(s.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-rule]'));
    let pick = 'winner', run: Agreement, rho: number[] = [], end: { ok: boolean; tick: number } | null = null;
    // at most the thesis's 100 timesteps, as in the race
    const HORIZON = 100;
    const reset = () => { run = agreement(net, RULES[pick], first, HORIZON); rho = [density(first)]; end = null; };
    function paintGrid() {
      const st = stage(grid); if (!st) return;
      const { ctx, t } = st, cell = st.w / side, a = run.state;
      ctx.fillStyle = t.panel; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, st.w, st.h); ctx.fillStyle = t.ink;
      for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) if (a[i]) ctx.fillRect((i % side) * cell, Math.floor(i / side) * cell, cell - 1.5, cell - 1.5);
    }
    function paintTrace() {
      const st = stage(trace); if (!st) return;
      const { t } = st, T = Math.max(60, rho.length), q: Plot = { x: 150, y: 10, w: st.w - 170, h: st.h - 100, x0: 0, x1: T, y0: 0, y1: 1 };
      axes(st, q, { xticks: [[0, '0'], [T, String(T)]], yticks: [[0, '0'], [1, '1']], xname: 'timestep', yname: 'share on' });
      curve(st, q, () => 0.5, t.line, 2, [6, 8]);
      st.ctx.strokeStyle = t.accent; st.ctx.lineWidth = 3; st.ctx.beginPath();
      rho.forEach((v, i) => { const X = px(q, i), Y = py(q, v); if (i) st.ctx.lineTo(X, Y); else st.ctx.moveTo(X, Y); });
      st.ctx.stroke();
      if (end) label(st, end.ok ? `all the same from timestep ${end.tick}` : end.tick < HORIZON ? `stuck from timestep ${end.tick}` : `no agreement in ${HORIZON} timesteps`, st.w - 10, 24, { size: 28, align: 'right', color: end.ok ? t.accent : t.danger, weight: 600, bg: true });
    }
    // three rounds a second; it stops when the verdict is in
    const ticks = ticker(s, 3, () => {
      if (end) return true;
      end = run.next(); rho.push(density(run.state));
      return !!end;
    }, () => { paintGrid(); paintTrace(); });
    const mark = () => buttons.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b.dataset.rule === pick)));
    const gridBtn = s.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-simbtn]')!;
    // another rule: back to the same start, and what was started runs on
    const restart = () => { const going = !!gridBtn.dataset.run; ticks.play(false); reset(); paintGrid(); paintTrace(); if (going) ticks.play(true); };
    buttons.forEach((b) => b.addEventListener('click', () => { if (b.dataset.rule === pick) return; pick = b.dataset.rule!; mark(); restart(); }));
    // another rewiring: the same nodes in the same states on the new links; before ▶ nothing else changes, after it the
    // run goes on, and one that was stuck tries again, up to the horizon (an agreement stays one)
    slider.addEventListener('input', () => {
      if (!rewire()) return;
      run.rewire(net);
      if (!gridBtn.dataset.run || run.round >= HORIZON) return;
      if (end && !end.ok) { end = null; ticks.play(true); }
    });
    reset();
    sim(gridBtn, 1, { reset() { ticks.play(false); reset(); paintGrid(); paintTrace(); }, play() { ticks.play(true); } });
    slide(s, { enter() { pick = 'winner'; mark(); slider.value = '-0.7'; rewire(); reset(); }, leave() { ticks.play(false); } });
    onSize(grid, paintGrid); onSize(trace, paintTrace);
  }
</script>
